\f1\fs22 Alexander, his bravado swollen by his mother\'92s unceasing support, barely acknowledged Tony stricken and terrified\'97perhaps a glance; maybe the wry, triumphant curl of his lip was all the confirmation he\'92d afford Tony. 
\f2\i I\'92ve won Sarah, and you\'92ve lost. 
\f1\i0 For a moment, out a window, Sarah looked through him with vague eyes, like a ghost from a mirror. Losing Sarah was like a shard shearing away from the flint of Tony\'92s soul. He felt raw; the exposed rock-face of his emotions bled like a crushed beetroot. He felt the stretching centimetres between them intensify the tension in his nerves until they snapped when the cavalcade disappeared from sight.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He fell to his knees and wept. With great reverence, a group of Elvi lifted him up, and he succumbed to their guidance until he found a reserve of determination from somewhere between his aching head, his clamped incisors, and the pit in his stomach. He shook off the Elvi\'92s guiding hands and staggered with leaden legs into the ruins of the mansion.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He refused to leave for days. He sat on the floor at the top of the stairs and dangled his legs into gaping space where Sarah\'92s room had been. He was mostly silent; occasionally his moaned, and counted as a blessing when tears would come. No one who took shifts in standing vigil was sure he ever slept. Very little of the sandwiches they placed at his side were consumed; he rarely drank. Several pairs of Dr John and Dr Nick attended and tried to persuade him to take medication. Sometimes they decided to administer injections, but were pushed away by other Elvi with thin lips and emphatic shakes of their heads.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He responded to none of of their encouragements or cajolery, none of the performances of his favorite songs, none of the writings of the Great Teacher. They tried arguing over the lyrics of certain songs, like they once would, before he gave them the gift of his words, hoping they might trigger his talents. But Tony remained mute, in an altered state where all he imagined was Sarah at the top of the stairs wearing an oversized tee, the way she did that first time when Tony found his voice. 
\f2\i \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 Evolve me tender, Evolve me strong, 
\f1\i0 he heard in his reverie, and imagined again a\cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
n orange glow over Sarah\'92s belly,  which afforded him supernatural vision of Little Clinton cradled in her womb. He sobbed and almost choked. Not only had Sarah abandoned him, but Little Clinton as well. Tony never reflected on it much before, but he always felt as if Sarah\'92s growing baby inspired him, and his absence now rendered Tony\'92s creativity barren. He blew his nose on his sleeve, the lapsed into stillness again, into his morbid meditation.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Great Teacher,\'92 an Elvi said. Tony only half heard, as if the speaker was at the top of a deep cave. \'91Great Teacher,\'92 She said again. \'91Please, just sing how you are feeling now \'85 let it out. There\'92s nothing more authentic than an artist expressing his pain.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tony heard only non-sensical syllables, and the Elvi retreated in meek failure.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The next day, as the sun rose and cast narrow, warm beams through the sieve of the wreckage, and rekindled an ember of Tony\'92s will to live. Whether his mind had allowed the words of the Elvi from the night before to be unscrambled, or it was just time, there were words he wanted to say\'97no, words he wanted to sing. And so he surrendered, and gave breath and a croaky throat to a muse more his than the Elvi\'92s.
